<section class="article-header wrap-article">
    <p class="section-label">Opinion</p>
    <h1>The Government Is Pricing Workers Out of Jobs</h1>
    <div class="byline-row">
        <img src="/assets/images/grace-pariser-avatar.webp" alt="Grace Pariser" class="byline-avatar">
        <p class="byline">By Grace Pariser &middot; September 2026</p>
    </div>
</section>

<section class="section wrap-article" style="border-top:none;">
    <div class="prose dropcap">
        <p>On 25 August, the government's consultation on guaranteed hours closed, having spent the summer asking businesses how to define "temporary need" for seasonal and project based work. The question exists because the government already knows what its own reform will do to an employer who takes on staff for three months over the summer and has nothing to offer them afterwards. It hasn't yet decided how to stop that outcome. In the meantime, two other changes to how businesses employ people take effect on 1 October, and a fourth is already live: an employment tribunal system taking more claims than ever. None of these were designed to make employing someone harder. Taken together, that's exactly what they do.</p>
        <p>The guaranteed hours reform is the clearest example of good intentions producing a bad incentive. Under the plan, anyone on a zero hours or low hours contract who works regularly over a reference period, likely to be twelve weeks, though even that isn't settled, has to be offered a permanent contract reflecting those hours. The aim is to stop businesses using casual contracts to dodge the security a regular worker deserves, and there are employers who've done exactly that, so the aim isn't wrong. But a business with genuinely seasonal work, a fortnight either side of twelve weeks, now has two options. Offer permanent hours it can't actually sustain once the season ends, or manage someone's hours down, or end the arrangement early, so the obligation never triggers. The second option is obviously worse for the worker the policy was meant to protect, and it's the one most businesses will take, because it's the only one that doesn't put next year's hiring at risk.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="pull-quote">
        <p>None of these were designed to make employing someone harder. Taken together, that's exactly what they do.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose">
        <p>That reform sits on top of changes that are already close to landing. From 1 October, employers become liable for right to work breaches committed by a subcontractor's staff, even where the individual has never been on their own payroll, a cleaner working through an agency being the obvious example. The same day, employers have to give every new starter a standalone document, in specified wording, explaining that they have the right to join a trade union, before their first day. Both are manageable in isolation. Together with the existing duty to record working hours and holiday for every employee every week, and a minimum wage regime where fifteen unpaid minutes over the week is a criminal offence rather than a fine, they add up to a genuinely complicated set of obligations for a business that just wants to hire someone to answer the phones.</p>
        <p>I'll concede the point most often made in response to this: none of these protections are unreasonable individually. Workers should know they can join a union. Businesses should be accountable for who works on their premises, even through a subcontractor. Nobody should be paid below minimum wage by fifteen minutes or fifteen pounds. I don't disagree with a single one of these principles, and I'd be uncomfortable arguing that any single measure, read on its own, is wrong.</p>
        <p>The problem is that nobody is adding up the total cost of all of this at once, and employers are the ones left to do it. Employing someone in the UK was already expensive before National Insurance went up this year, and every one of these changes adds cost, risk, or both, on top of a wage bill that was rising anyway. A rational employer facing that total doesn't respond by treating staff better. It responds by hiring fewer of them, by leaning harder on contractors who sit outside some of these obligations, or by automating a role rather than filling it. That's not a failure of employer goodwill. It's what happens when the cost of a decision changes and the decision changes with it.</p>
        <p>The claims side of this makes the total worse, not better. Tribunal claims are up, helped along by press coverage of employment rights that's been near constant since the election, and by workers turning to AI to help build a case, which brings its own problem given how often that advice turns out to be wrong. A business can follow every rule correctly and still face a claim, and once it does, the sums rarely favour fighting it. Legal fees to defend a claim properly can run to thirty thousand pounds. Settling for a fraction of that, with no admission of wrongdoing, is simply the cheaper option, whatever the merits of the claim actually were. That's not justice for the worker with a genuine grievance. It's a tax on having employed anyone at all.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="pull-quote">
        <p>That's not justice for the worker with a genuine grievance. It's a tax on having employed anyone at all.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose">
        <p>None of this is really about worker protection failing on its own terms, incidentally. Each measure does roughly what it says it will do. The failure is in treating employment reform as a series of separate announcements rather than a single cost that lands on one employer's balance sheet at the same time. A government that wants more people in work needs to look at what it's asking employers to carry before it adds the next thing, not after the consultation on the last one has already closed.</p>
        <p>The reforms solve a political problem, being seen to strengthen workers' rights, rather than an employment problem, getting more people into work in the first place. The people paying for that gap are the ones who never get hired at all.</p>
    </div>
</section>

